<p>Downturns in the farm economy and easier access to larger shopping centers—particularly after the Expressway Bridge connected Mandan with Kirkwood Mall in south Bismarck—led to many retail-store closures in Mandan. Downtown businesses were especially hard hit, and the discovery of an oil plume floating atop the groundwater compounded their difficulties. The actual and perceived effects of the underground oil discouraged efforts to renovate or repurpose abandoned buildings, even as other small communities across the Great Plains confronted similar commercial changes.</p>

<p>Another oil boom-and-bust cycle also affected Mandan. Crude-oil and gasoline prices peaked early in the decade after a second worldwide oil embargo. Just as Amoco Oil Company's Mandan refinery underwent a major expansion in 1981–82 to eliminate lead compounds from gasoline, oil prices and profits plummeted in the middle of the decade, putting pressure on operations and staffing.</p>

<h3>1984</h3>

<p>The Mandan Park Board approved construction of new rodeo grounds on the former landfill south of Memorial Highway. The estimated cost was $200,000 for the grandstand, plus $56,000 for water and sewer service. The original fairground and rodeo stands would be removed to make way for additional softball diamonds.</p>

<p>After six months of traffic delays, a new Highway 6 bridge opened across the Heart River. The City Commission designated it the Custer Bridge.</p>

<p>A McDonald's restaurant opened on East Main Street in December. Four video-rental businesses were also established. This new form of entertainment allowed customers to rent movies and a video player, take the equipment home, and connect it to their televisions. A package with the player and four movies cost $24 per day. Among the new stores was Feist TV and Electronics, which {% person "andrew-feist" %}Andrew Feist{% endperson %} moved from Bismarck.</p>

<p>The year also marked the inauguration of Mandan's Care and Share program, which helped local families facing hardship. In its first year, the program collected more than $15,000 and assisted 283 families.</p>

<h3>1985</h3>

<p>The four-lane Bismarck Expressway Bridge opened across the Missouri River, connecting the Interstate 94 Business Loop in east Mandan with south Bismarck. Although it shortened commutes for residents of both cities, it also diverted traffic from Memorial Highway. Mandan shoppers increasingly shifted their business—and sales-tax dollars—to Bismarck's large regional shopping centers. Mandan's J. C. Penney store, for example, closed in April after 65 years in the city. The Gambles store closed in December.</p>

<h3>1986</h3>

<p>The loss of Main Street retailers continued when Collins Department Store closed in January. The site had continuously housed a clothing store since Tubbs &amp; Cummins Clothiers opened there in 1881, the year Mandan was incorporated.</p>

<h3>1987</h3>

