  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1980s/154-auditorium-mandan-high.jpg" alt="Mandan High School auditorium">
    <figcaption>Mandan High School auditorium</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Mandan High School's new 600-seat auditorium opened for performances in January. Photographer {% person "george-masseth-jr" %}George Masseth Jr.{% endperson %} was elected to a two-year term on the National Council of Professional Photographers of America, an organization with more than 15,000 members.</p>

<h3>1988</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1980s/499-cloverdale-downtown-wedge-shot-1950s.jpg" alt="Historic image from the &quot;Oil&quot; Boom and Bust 1980-1989 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On March 11, the Mandan Creamery and Produce plant at Main Street and Fourth Avenue NW closed after 90 years of operation at the site. The company, now known as Cloverdale Meats, transferred production to a new $6 million facility in northwest Mandan and refocused its business on pork products.</p>

<p>The decline of downtown retail continued with the closure of several more landmark stores, including Dakota Four Seasons Clothing and Mushik Shoe Store. {% person "john-mushik" %}John Mushik{% endperson %} came to Mandan in 1909 and opened a business on East Main Street. In 1913, the cobbler moved to larger quarters at 110 West Main Street and began selling men's shoes exclusively. He shared the business with tailors {% person "a-s-brazada" %}A. S. Brazda{% endperson %} and {% person "mike-gish" %}Mike Gish{% endperson %}. His son {% person "john-mushik-jr", "John Mushik Jr." %}John Jr.{% endperson %} joined the trade in 1917. Mrs. Mushik ran the store while her sons served during World War II. {% person "john-mushik-iii", "John Mushik III" %}John III{% endperson %} operated it until his retirement in 1962. Longtime employee {% person "jerry-hoff" %}Jerry Hoff{% endperson %} then bought the business and ran it until it closed in March 1988.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1980s/150-dance-party-usa-poster-1988.jpg" alt="Historic image from the &quot;Oil&quot; Boom and Bust 1980-1989 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The June 24 episode of USA Network's <em>Dance Party USA</em> featured an event staged at Mandan High School. Students sent more than 600,000 postcards to win the "Another Chance to Dance" hosting contest. They packed the school gymnasium to see host {% person "andy-gury" %}Andy Gury{% endperson %}, teenage star {% person "debbie-gibson" %}Debbie Gibson{% endperson %}, and 12 of the show's 24 regular dancers perform. A highlight came when Gibson returned to the stage wearing a Mandan High T-shirt for her final song.</p>

<p>On October 15, the Mandan Elks held the grand opening of their new home at 1408 Collins Avenue. The club had more than 1,000 members. It closed on April 22, 1995.</p>

<h3>1989</h3>

<p>The Mandan Jaycee Rodeo was held for the last time at the Syndicate Rodeo Grounds over the Fourth of July weekend. Hot-air balloon rides were a special feature of the event.</p>

<p>In July, the Joersz family celebrated the opening of a new 38,000-square-foot building on West Main Street for their Bill's SuperValu store. The grocery business, previously located at 511 First Street NW, had operated since 1976.</p>
